Offset: the offsets of two faces that meet along a crease split into
several edges, intersected once for all of them, trimmed once and oriented
in each face as their own edge is there (mitcad#2383).

The case: a cone standing on a ring, with notches cut up from the ring's
top through the cone's wall (their floors in the ring's top), shelled
inside with Sharp corners (the intersection join). The cone's foot, the
crease between the cone and the ring's top, is split by the notches into
arcs and by the cone's seam once more; the offsets of the two faces are
intersected once for all of those arcs, in one circle, which the offset
then splits and trims for each run of arcs. The offset failed (no result,
or a solid with a notch whose walls the inside of the cone ran across):

- BRepOffset_Inter3d::ConnexIntByInt: the copy of the circle made for each
  further run of arcs (each localized shared part) went to the offsets of
  both faces with one orientation, though the circle is stored for the two
  each with its own: the copy ran against the wire of one of them. It now
  goes to each face as the circle itself is there.
- TrimEdges: a piece shared by the images of several edges (the arcs on
  both sides of the seam) was trimmed again for each of them, as
  NCollection_IndexedMap::Add gives an index, not whether the piece is
  new. A second trim of a piece cut at its curve's origin (TrimEdge,
  mitcad#2050) took the run of arcs the other way round the circle, across
  every other notch. Each piece is now trimmed once.
- TrimEdge: the second piece of such a cut went to the faces with the
  orientation the edge had where it was cut, not as each face has the
  edge: the cone's offset then closed its foot loop alone, its seam and
  top left out. It now goes to each face as the edge is there.
- IsOverOrigins (which arcs of a circle lie over the edges it is the image
  of, mitcad#2050): the projection of an arc's middle onto an edge's curve
  stored only over the edge's range (trimmed, or not periodic) gives the
  farthest point where the nearest one lies outside the range; the arc
  across a notch's gap was so taken for one over the arcs, and the circle
  not trimmed at all. Only a nearest point (a local minimum of the
  distance) inside the range counts now.
- TrimEdges: the image of an edge that comes in a chain of pieces (the
  intersection of the cone's offset with a notch's round wall's, in two
  branches meeting at its apex) has each piece trimmed between its
  neighbours' vertices on it and the vertex it shares with the next piece
  of the chain; a piece with one neighbour's vertex was not trimmed at all
  and the wall's offset was left open.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2383.
